    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data regarding thermal management in high-performance braking systems. Specifically, I'm comparing the heat dissipation rates of carbon-ceramic discs versus semi-metallic pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles. Can you explain the primary mechanisms of heat soak in these materials and how they contribute to brake fade during heavy use?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Heat soak in braking systems is a complex process, but it's fundamentally about how quickly the generated thermal energy moves *through* the braking components. In carbon-ceramic discs, the material itself has a relatively high thermal conductivity, which helps it shed heat away from the friction interface quickly, although the surrounding structure still absorbs some heat. For semi-metallic pads, the heat soak is often more pronounced because their composition can trap heat more readily, leading to localized hotspots.\n\nThis heat soak contributes to brake fade because as the material temperature rises, several things happen, like the thermal degradation of the friction material's binder or the change in the coefficient of friction itself. Essentially, the material starts behaving less predictably, which is the mechanical manifestation of the thermal stress. High temperatures can also cause gases to be released from the pad material, which interferes with the pad-rotor interface, leading to reduced braking efficiency.",
      "source": "target",
