    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That comparison to vibrating pebbles helps visualize the instability. You also mentioned how the components can start behaving \"less predictably\" and how the mechanical contact mechanics become \"erratic.\" In some of the telemetry we're seeing, there are these sudden, almost mischievous-feeling spikes in temperature and irregular deceleration patterns that don't seem to follow a clean linear degradation. It's almost like the system is resisting the driver's input. Why would a mechanical system exhibit such seemingly erratic or \"uncooperative\" behavior under stress?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a mechanical system exhibits seemingly erratic or \"uncooperative\" behavior under extreme stress, it usually points toward a transition into a non-linear failure mode, often related to energy dissipation dynamics. These sudden spikes are rarely random; they are the system reaching a critical threshold where one failure mechanism takes over from another.\n\nIn braking, this erratic behavior can be linked to the onset of localized material phase changes or the sudden onset of gas pressurization within the pad structure. If the friction material reaches a point where it begins to outgas rapidly—releasing trapped volatile compounds—this gas can temporarily cushion the pad against the rotor, causing a sudden, brief drop in friction (a \"fade spike\"). Conversely, if a localized area overheats severely, the material might undergo a rapid exothermic reaction, causing a sudden, sharp temperature spike.\n\nThese events are not a conscious resistance, but rather the physical manifestation of the material structure failing to maintain its intended stable thermodynamic state under the immense load. It’s the system momentarily becoming *too* hot for its designed chemistry to handle gracefully.",
      "source": "target",
